---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Byline from '../../components/Byline.astro';
import SpeciesCard from '../../components/SpeciesCard.astro';
import { getSpecies } from '../../lib/data';
import { personLd } from '../../lib/site';

const published = new Date('2026-10-13');
const title = "Could Colombia's cocaine hippos evolve into a new species?";
const description =
  "Escobar's hippos are isolated, inbred and living in a world without droughts or lions. Could they become a new species, and how long would it take? What evolutionary biology says.";
const site = Astro.site!;
const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: title,
    description,
    url: new URL(Astro.url.pathname, site).href,
    image: new URL('/og/hippopotamus-amphibius-magdalenae.png', site).href,
    datePublished: published.toISOString(),
    author: personLd(site),
  },
  {
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Cocaine hippos', item: new URL('/hippos/', site).href },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'A new species?' },
    ],
  },
];
const species = await getSpecies();
const examples = ['hippopotamus-amphibius-magdalenae', 'hylopotamus-nanus', 'sirenopotamus-magdalenae']
  .map((id) => species.find((s) => s.id === id))
  .filter((s) => s !== undefined);
---
<Base title={title} description={description} ogImage="/og/hippopotamus-amphibius-magdalenae.png" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <article class="wrap prose">
    <p class="eyebrow"><a href="/hippos/">Cocaine hippos</a> · Evolution</p>
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <Byline published={published} />
    <p class="lead">
      Short answer: not in our lifetime, and probably not at all if Colombia removes them. But if they stay, the
      hippos have almost everything evolution needs to make something new.
    </p>

    <h2>What it takes to become a new species</h2>
    <p>
      A new species usually forms when part of a population is cut off from the rest, so that its genes no longer
      mix with them, and is then shaped by different conditions for long enough. The Colombian hippos meet the first
      condition completely. They are thousands of kilometres from Africa and will never breed with African hippos again.
    </p>
    <p>
      The second condition is time. Hippos live long and breed slowly, with a generation time of roughly a decade. For
      large mammals, becoming a distinct species typically takes hundreds of thousands to a few million years. Visible
      changes in size or behaviour can come much faster, within thousands of years.
    </p>

    <h2>What is already different in Colombia</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>A tiny founder population.</strong> All of them descend from a handful of animals, usually reported as
        one male and three females. Their genetic variation is therefore a small, random sample of the species. This
        <em>founder effect</em> can push a population in new directions quickly.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No drought.</strong> African hippos are limited by dry seasons that shrink their pools and grazing. The
        Magdalena valley has water all year round.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No natural enemies for adults.</strong> There are no lions and no Nile crocodiles. The caimans are far
        too small to take adults.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Rapid breeding.</strong> The 2021–2022 census found that more than a third of the animals were young.
        One hypothesis is that females mature earlier than in Africa.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>People.</strong> Hunting, fences, roads and boats favour hippos that are shy, nocturnal and keep their
        distance. This is selection too.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Is there a precedent?</h2>
    <p>
      Yes, several. The hippo family has repeatedly produced dwarf forms when populations were isolated. The pygmy hippo
      lives in the rainforests of West Africa today, and extinct dwarf hippos lived on Cyprus, Crete, Sicily, Malta and
      Madagascar. Hippos are also the closest living relatives of whales, which shows how far this branch of mammals can
      go once it commits to the water.
    </p>
    <p>
      Researchers have also pointed out that South America lost its giant herbivores at the end of the last ice age.
      The hippos partly fill roles that have been empty for about 12,000 years. An empty ecological role is exactly the
      kind of opportunity that drives evolution.
    </p>

    <h2>Why it probably won't happen</h2>
    <p>
      A population this small and inbred is vulnerable to disease and genetic problems. More importantly, Colombia
      classifies the hippos as an invasive species, and sterilisation, relocation and culling are all on the table. The
      evolutionary future described on this site is a thought experiment, not a forecast and not an argument for leaving
      the hippos alone.
    </p>

    <h2>So what could they become?</h2>
    <p>
      If the thought experiment runs for twenty million years, the possibilities open up. A smaller Magdalena hippo
      could appear within ten thousand years, a forest dwarf and a manatee-like river grazer within a few million, and
      perhaps even a marine hippo grazing on the Caribbean seagrass beds.
    </p>
  </article>

  <section class="wrap">
    <div class="grid">{examples.map((s) => <SpeciesCard species={s} />)}</div>
    <p class="cta">
      <a class="btn" href="/hippos/present/">Walk the timeline</a>
      <a class="btn secondary" href="/hippos/#faq">Facts and FAQ</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .cta { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.75rem; margin-top: 1.5rem; }
  li { margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
</style>
